Following [Theorem 2.12](1972_Theorem_2_12.md), Scott steps back from the proof to compare two pictures of the same equivalence.

The **lattice approach** to injective spaces gives a completely “internal” characterization: the types are complete lattices; **way-below** (`≪`, [Definition 2.3](../WayBelow/1972_ContinuousLattice.md)) is defined lattice-theoretically by

$$
x \ll y \quad \text{iff} \quad \text{whenever } y \sqsubseteq \bigsqcup Z \text{ and } Z \subseteq D \text{ is directed,}
$$

then $x \sqsubseteq z$ for some $z \in Z$; and every $y \in D$ satisfies

$$y = \bigsqcup \{ x \in D : x \ll y \}.$$

That makes $D$ a continuous lattice with the sets $\{ y \in D : x \ll y \}$ as a basis for the topology. Such $T_0$-spaces are injective, every injective space arises this way, and the lattice structure is uniquely determined by the topology. Furthermore, as in the proof of 2.11, injectivity can be exhibited by an explicit extension formula.

The **retract approach** should also be considered. Cartesian powers $\mathbb{O}^I$ are simple: as lattices they are Boolean algebras of all subsets of $I$. The topology has as a basis the classes of sets containing given finite sets (the *weak topology*, cf. Nerode (1959)). A continuous function

$$j : \mathcal{P}I \to \mathcal{P}I$$

is one of “finite character”, so

$$j(X) = \bigcup \{ j(F) : F \subseteq X \}$$

where $X \subseteq I$ and $F$ ranges over *finite* sets. Such $j$ is a *retraction* iff it is *idempotent* ($j \circ j = j$), so the range is the set of fixed points. As we have seen in [Idempotent fixed points](1972_IdempotentFixedPoint.md),

$$D = \{ X \in \mathcal{P}I : j(X) = X \}$$

is a continuous lattice (under $\subseteq$ here), and *every* continuous lattice is isomorphic to one obtained this way. This is a representation theorem, but Scott warns it is not very helpful for proofs.

Finally, any space (any finite family of spaces $X$, $Y$, …) embeds as a *subspace* of some continuous lattice $D$. Since $D$ is injective, every continuous $f : X \to Y$ extends to some $\bar{f} : D \to D$; maps $D \to D$ therefore encode continuous maps on all subspaces—motivation for [Function spaces](../1972_3_Function_spaces.md).
